Because the AI Studio workspace pushes through its built-in GitHub sync rather than terminal git, pushing to GitHub and GitLab happens in two steps:
1. Push from AI Studio to GitHub
In the AI Studio toolbar at the top right, click the GitHub icon (push/sync to repository).
Review the changed files, enter a commit message (e.g., feat: initial deposit banner and responsive dashboard fixes), and click Commit & Push.
Note: If the GitHub button shows "Nothing to push", refresh the page or make a trivial save if needed—once synced, your changes are live on github/main.
2. Sync GitHub → GitLab (~/VeritasOrg-sync)
Open your terminal in ~/VeritasOrg-sync and run one of these options depending on what you want on GitLab (origin/main):
Option A: Sync changes cleanly on top of GitLab's existing history (Safest if GitLab has commits GitHub doesn't)
Bash
cd ~/VeritasOrg-sync
git fetch github
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git checkout github/main -- \
  client/src/components/initial-deposit-banner.tsx \
  client/src/components/layout-shell.tsx \
  client/src/components/transaction-amount-balance.tsx \
  client/src/pages/dashboard.tsx \
  server/fees/ui.test.ts
git add -A
git commit -m "feat: initial deposit banner and responsive dashboard fixes"
git push origin main
Option B: Make GitLab match GitHub (github/main) 1:1 (Only if GitHub is your single source of truth)
If git push origin main gets rejected as non-fast-forward because the two histories diverged, you can replace origin/main with github/main cleanly:
Bash
cd ~/VeritasOrg-sync
git fetch github
git checkout main
git reset --hard github/main
git push --force-with-lease origin main
(Use Option A unless you are sure there are no GitLab-only commits you still need.)
